Files use kdeconnect.share.request with a filename and an optional payload.
An empty file has a filename and no payload. Incoming files are saved in the
configured download directory and renamed when the destination already exists.
The optional sha256 field contains the lowercase SHA-256 digest of the file.
This version computes it before sending and verifies it after writing the
received file. A mismatch is reported as an error and the file is deleted.
Hashing runs in background threads. Older clients can receive ordinary files
but do not verify this field; files from clients without it remain supported.
The sender's upload completion is not an acknowledgement of receiver verification.

Folders require both devices to advertise kdeconnect.share.request.folder.
Each folder has a UUID folderId, folderName, entryCount, and ordered entryIndex
starting at zero. Each entry uses this packet type with a relativePath and a
directory boolean. Entry zero is the root directory with an empty relativePath.
Directories have no payload; regular files include a filename, payload (unless
empty), and sha256. All entries go through the existing upload/download queues.
The receiver reserves a new root directory, renaming the whole root on collision.
Directory entries precede their children, preserving empty folders. Paths must
stay inside that root and may not traverse symbolic links or Windows junctions.
Links, special files, case-only name collisions, and names incompatible with
Windows are rejected during preparation. Transfers are limited to one million
entries per root and a nesting depth of 256. Failed transfers can leave partial
folders; there is no resume or automatic rollback of previously received files.

The app's Share Files and Folders dialog lets users add multiple files and
folders to one selection. Add Folder can be used repeatedly before Send.
The CLI also accepts mixed selections, for example on Windows:

    kdeconnect-cli --device DEVICE_ID --share "C:\Folder A" "C:\Folder B" "C:\file.txt"

Text uses a text field instead of a payload and is copied to the clipboard.
Web links use a url field and open in the default browser.
